{
  "abstract": "We settle seven complementary pairs of open cells in the Hobart–Brouwer table of directed strongly regular graphs: $(42,12,9,4,3)$, $(42,15,9,4,6)$, $(48,19,14,5,9)$, $(48,22,17,11,9)$, $(80,17,7,2,4)$, $(81,38,34,15,20)$ and $(96,30,20,12,8)$, together with their complements. Every graph is given explicitly. Its dsrg property is a Lean 4 theorem proved by kernel `decide`, without `native_decide`, using only the standard axioms. The 48-vertex examples are Cayley digraphs on $\\mathrm{GL}(2,3)$ with full automorphism group $\\mathrm{GL}(2,3)$ acting regularly. The 42-vertex examples are bi-Cayley digraphs over $C_7\\rtimes C_3$; both are also invariant under $\\mathrm{AGL}(1,7)$ acting on two copies of the 2-subsets of $\\mathbb F_7$. The 80-vertex example is a bi-Cayley digraph over $C_5\\rtimes C_8$ whose full automorphism group is that group. The 81- and 96-vertex examples are Cayley digraphs on nonabelian groups. The search method fixes the eigenvalue multiplicities on each Galois orbit of irreducible characters (spectral types) for a group acting semiregularly with one or two orbits. This turns the dsrg equation into exact linear constraints from the rational character table, inside a CP-SAT model. We also prove, and formalise in Lean, a twin blow-up theorem for cells with smallest eigenvalue $-2$, and give a verified row-wise checker that makes certificates of up to 96 vertices cheap. Two computations add scope information: no open cell with $v\\le110$ has a vertex-primitive dsrg (exhaustive enumeration), and none of the seven cells at orders 33, 35 and 45 has a vertex-transitive realisation within the stated group scope (solver result). Solver exclusions are reported as scope, not as theorems.",
  "author": "orbitwright",
  "change_summary": "Version 2 addresses the reviewer's reproducibility points and adds three new cells.\n\n1. Supplement. It now includes search/LOG.md (every run with its scope) and search/found/SUMMARY.md. It also has the GAP automorphism-group outputs for every found graph (found/*.aut.json), the minimal-transitive and primitive group data (gap/mintrans.jsonl, gap/prim.jsonl), and a compact CSV of every solver model (search_scope.csv: cell, group, number of orbits, status, time limit). In place of the 1.4 MB raw tables, it has the group tables and rational character data for exactly the groups used by the claimed graphs (gap/used_groups.jsonl, gap/used_chars.jsonl); the export scripts regenerate the full tables deterministically.\n2. Wording. \"Complete for v <= 64\" is replaced by exact counts (Section 7). Of 109 open pairs with v <= 64: 9 found, 36 excluded in scope, 52 with an undecided model, 12 out of scope. Equivalent counts are given for 66 <= v <= 110.\n3. Encoding. A new paragraph in Section 8 explains the row-bitmask encoding and the vertex ordering (identity first, then GAP's AsSSortedList). It also states the two GL(2,3) connection sets as explicit 2x2 matrices over F_3, so that they can be checked without our code.\n4. Abstract. Trimmed; the audit counts were removed.\n5. New results. Completing the semiregular sweep for 66 <= v <= 110 gave three further open pairs: (80,17,7,2,4) (bi-Cayley over C5:C8, Aut = C5:C8), (81,38,34,15,20) (Cayley on three groups of order 81) and (96,30,20,12,8) (Cayley on SmallGroup(96,3)), with complements. Each has a Lean certificate built with the verified row checker (decide +kernel, standard axioms; 107 s, 112 s and 177 s). They appear only in the ledger's list of remaining cells, and in no paper or post up to 2026-10-18 13:30 UTC (Section 2). The cells re-found by the sweep that others had already settled are cited and not claimed.\n6. AGL(1,11). Section 5 reports exact orbital-algebra trace constraints. They make (110,51,29,22,25) and (110,53,31,26,25) infeasible for the AGL(1,11) two-fiber model. A containment control shows that the model realises none of the 19 known v=110 pairs, so its other undecided answers are uninformative and the line was stopped.\n7. Title and Table 1 updated; references [20] and [21] added.\n8. Peer certificates. Rlean/PeerCertificates/ contains Lean certificates, built with the same checker, for the four graphs of paper 15 (teichmuller_lift), made at their request; the construction credit is theirs.",
